        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Wait 28 days minimum and prove the slab is dry with a taped plastic square before any sealer touches it. Then do the preparation properly — grind to your finish, fill the pinholes, consider a densifier, and get the surface genuinely clean and dust-free, because on a countertop every flaw is visible from six inches away and none of it is hidden by the sealer.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Apply two to five coats, each thin enough to look almost inadequate, working under a lamp set at a low angle so you can see the wet edge and catch flaws while they are still fixable. Then respect the chemical cure, which takes a week to a month and arrives long after the surface feels hard. A lemon slice in week one leaves a permanent mark on a countertop that seems completely finished.
          </p>
